  "query_preview": "..High pressure over the southern Plains today will \nbe shifting westward through the end of the week, allowing more of\na southwesterly flow to spread into the area. Afternoon PWATs \ntoday ranged from 0.9 inches across far northern and northeast \nUtah and southwest Wyoming to 1.2 inches across southwest Utah. \nThis allowed scattered showers and thunderstorms to develop over \nthe area again, most of which should gradually diminish \novernight. However, isolated rain showers will remain possible \nthrough the night. \n\nFor Thursday, PWATs will remain largely similar to today, then \ntrend slightly downward for Friday. However, in between the ridge \naxis to the east and a mean trough developing to the west, a \nseries of shortwave weather disturbances is progged to eject \nacross Utah Thursday and Friday. Larger-scale lift and increased \njet support will favor more widespread and stronger storms, which \nwill in turn bring an increased potential for heavy rain, small \nhail, and gusty winds. Thursday should generally feature the most \nactive weather day this week, as thunderstorm activity may \ndecrease a bit for Friday owing to the moisture trends.\n\nFor Saturday and Sunday, PWATs continue to trend downward, to 1\ninch or less in most areas. As a result, convective activity will\ndecrease for the weekend, although likely not completely dry, as \nsome terrain-based showers are still anticipated. Thereafter, \nthere is still some model spread in relation to the ridge position\nfor next week and whether significant moisture will return. There\nare some indications that moisture may return as early as Monday.\nLatest deterministics, however, are not very promising in that \nregard, and NBM trends currently keep PWATs and PoPs on the lower \nside through at least the middle of next week. After cooler \ntemperatures through Friday, temperatures will start to gradually \nclimb again over the weekend, returning closer to climatological \nnormals by the early to middle part of next week, especially if \nthe airmass does remain on the drier side.",
